        ("What does this refer to inside a regular function event handler?", "Inside a regular function handler, this refers to event.currentTarget (the element that owns the listener).", "Intermediate", "Concept", "btn.addEventListener('click', function() {\n  console.log(this === btn); // true\n});", "What does this refer to in an arrow function listener?"),
        ("What does this refer to inside an arrow function event listener?", "Inside an arrow function, this retains the lexical enclosing scope (usually window or module), NOT the clicked element.", "Intermediate", "Concept", "btn.addEventListener('click', (e) => {\n  console.log(e.currentTarget === btn); // Use e.currentTarget instead of this\n});", "Why should you use e.currentTarget in arrow handlers?"),
        ("How do you remove an event listener using removeEventListener?", "Call element.removeEventListener(type, handlerFunction) with the exact same function reference and matching capture options.", "Easy", "Practical", "btn.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);", "Can anonymous arrow functions be removed?"),
        ("Why can't anonymous functions attached via addEventListener be removed with removeEventListener?", "removeEventListener requires the exact memory reference of the handler function. Anonymous functions create a new reference that cannot be referenced later.", "Easy", "Concept", "btn.addEventListener('click', () => {}); // Cannot be removed!", "How do you solve this?"),
