        ("What is the difference between .position() and .offset() in jQuery?", ".offset() returns coordinates (top, left) relative to the document. .position() returns coordinates relative to the offset parent element.", "Intermediate", "Comparison", "const pagePos = $('#box').offset(); // { top: 120, left: 50 }\nconst parentPos = $('#box').position(); // relative to offset parent", "Which one can be SET as well as read?"),
        ("Can .offset() be used to set an element's position on the page?", "Yes, .offset({ top: 100, left: 200 }) sets the element's position relative to the document.", "Intermediate", "Practical", "$('#tooltip').offset({ top: e.pageY + 10, left: e.pageX + 10 });", "Can .position() be set?"),
        ("What does .scrollTop() do in jQuery?", ".scrollTop() gets or sets the vertical scroll position of the window or a scrollable element.", "Easy", "Practical", "$(window).scrollTop(0); // Scrolls window to top", "What does .scrollLeft() do?"),
