        ("What is the difference between .parent() and .parents() in jQuery?", ".parent() travels only a single level up to the immediate parent. .parents() travels all the way up through all ancestors to the root <html>.", "Easy", "Comparison", "$('#child').parents('.card');", "What does .parentsUntil() do?"),
        ("What does .parentsUntil(selector) do?", "It gets all ancestors up to, but not including, the element matched by the selector.", "Intermediate", "Concept", "$('#child').parentsUntil('#main-container');", "Does it include the selector element?"),
        ("What does .closest() do in jQuery?", ".closest(selector) traverses up through ancestors starting from the element itself until it finds a match for the selector.", "Intermediate", "Practical", "const $card = $(this).closest('.card');", "How does .closest differ from .parents?"),
        ("What are two key differences between .closest() and .parents() in jQuery?", "1. .closest() begins searching from the element itself; .parents() begins from the parent. 2. .closest() stops at the first match and returns 0 or 1 element; .parents() returns all matching ancestors.", "Intermediate", "Comparison", "", "Which is better for event delegation?"),
